In article <37176@ursa.bear.com>, 
halat@panther.bear.com (Jim Halat) said:

> I was referring to the phrase "free will of a person who might want to
> have read the paper has been overridden/negated without his consent."
> Both stealing [free newspapers from their distribution site] and
> banning [a book from a library] seem to have a similar effect on the
> reader.  I agree, however, that differences exist between the
> situations.

Right.  One big difference is this: with regard to the newspapers, the
would-be reader had a right to have a reasonable chance of acquiring a
copy of the newspaper in a manner appropriate with the intent of the
paper's distributor, while in the case of the book in the library, the
would-be reader had a right to have a reasonable chance of acquiring
(temporarily) a reading copy of the book _in a manner appropriate with
the intent of the person or institution which owns and operates the
library_.

In the former case, the only players with rights (the would-be reader
and the distributor) have the same goal in mind.  (This discussion, of
course, assumes the simplest case of the free- newspapers scenario: one
in which there is no other party with special rights.  Obviously, things
would be a lot less simple if there also was a controversy between the
newspaper distributor and the owner of the land upon which the
distributor wanted to maintain a distribution site.)  The newspaper
thieves are violating the right of the would-be reader while (arguably)
not exercising any legitimate right of their own, and the conflict has
arisen among the involved parties only because of the unrightful actions
of one of the parties.

In the latter case, it is not necessarily true that the would-be reader
and the library owner/operator have precisely the same goals in mind:
it's entirely possible that the reader's goal is to obtain from the
library a copy of the book while it is _not_ the library's goal to
maintain a reading copy of that book.  If this is the case, and if the
library can rightfully decline to maintain such a copy of the book
(e.g., because the book is inappropriate to the library's purpose, is
redundant, is too expensive, etc.), then a conflict has arisen between
the involved parties [here's the punch line] *absent* the unrightful act
of any party.

And where there is no unrightful action by any party, what's left is
merely a dispute over desires and privileges, not a conflict over
rights.

-- William December Starr <wdstarr@crl.com>


