Subject: Computer Pornography Hits British Schools
Date: Thu, 16 Jun 1994 20:22:28 -0500



LONDON -- Computer pornography is rife in Britain's
schools, an independent survey released Thursday said.

A report by the University of Central Lancashire in northern
England found that some pornographic discs are used on school
machines while others are passed around outside the classroom.

``This type of material is circulated amongst children in
secondary and, in some cases primary schools, throughout
Britain,'' the report said.

It added that over 30 percent of boys in secondary schools
and two percent in primary schools were aware of computer
pornography.

``This is abuse of technology,'' Vicki Merchant, the author
of the report, told the Guardian newspaper.

``It is very powerful and children are very impressionable
-- computing is addictive to some children. If you add these
factors together, it's good news for pornography,'' she added.

Merchant, who contacted 28,000 schools for the survey and
received 7,500 responses, said teachers thought most of the
illicit material came from the students' homes. Bulletin boards,
computer clubs and stores were also listed as sources.

Teachers who responded to the report suggested closer
supervision of school equipment, disciplinary action and more
parental control as ways of tackling the problem.


