In article <kadie.771215562@hal.cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@hal.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:

> p.s. I also dislike the idea of unmoderated newsgroups that restrict
> viewpoint.

matt@physics5.berkeley.edu (Matt Austern) writes:

>I really don't understand why, to be honest.
[...]

Two reasons, I guess.

First, enforcing a viewpoint restriction is very subjective and
difficult. I think that if some folks want such a restriction, they
should do the enforcement work themselves (via, for example, a
moderated newsgroup) rather than trying to get others to accept the
burden.

Second, I think I believe in a moral right-of-reply in open forums.  I
think that if you say in an open forum that house cats don't really
hurt wild birds that much, I have a moral right to report that house
cats are very destructive to many suburban ecosystems. If you right "I
wish people were more like cats", I think have a moral right to report
that tom cats will kill the kittens of another male if they can.

Why don't I see similar right of reply in moderated forums? Because I
see moderated forums as speaking with the moderator's voice and I
don't believe that people should (generally) be forced to speak
against their will.

- Carl
