	Let me just throw my two cents worth in here.  At a Federal
Government site, users have to sign a "canon of conduct" stating that
they will not do anything "illegal" on the system, whatever that means.
Among other things, I assume that this means that you will not divulge
government secrets to the communists, etc.  There is nothing, at this
time, about sending mail to newsgroups, but that is coming...

	System Administrators aren't even supposed to look at user's
mail files, except when there is a problem that prevents the user
from using mail.  I don't believe that there is any provision to
allow the Sys Admin to look at user mail if they have reason to believe
that the user is abusing the system, except maybe on a case-by-case
basis.

	We aren't even supposed to run LAN Analyzers unless we are
looking for a specific problem, i.e., looking for a bad segment on the
net.  Snooping on packets is not allowed.

	On the other hand, the network manager at another Government
agency wanted to see a copy of EVERY mail message entering or leaving
"his" systems.  He was in short told that what he wanted was illegal.


	It would be interesting to see what other organizations have
in place to prevent net abuse from occuring on their systems, and
what restrictions the system administrators have to prevent them
from doing their jobs.

